{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgsJFj3UxivH19mkUhBjm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Pdfzsx8cAH74EeXpWShbEg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Administrator\\.claude\\plans\\add-input-validation-to-temporal-quail.md","content":"# Plan: input validation for `expenses add`\n\n## Context\n`add` currently accepts any float (0, negatives, `nan`, `inf`) and any category string (including blank), and writes them straight to the CSV via `append_expense`. Goal: reject bad input at the argparse layer so it never reaches storage, using argparse's normal error path (stderr message, exit 2), the same path as the existing non-numeric amount case.\n\nScope: only `expenses/cli.py` and `tests/test_cli.py`. No changes to models/storage/report, list/summary, or CSV loading.\n\n## Approach: argparse `type=` validators in `expenses/cli.py`\nDo validation inside argparse type functions. When one raises `argparse.ArgumentTypeError`, argparse prints `usage... error: argument amount: <msg>` to stderr and calls `sys.exit(2)` during `parse_args`, so `main` never gets to `append_expense` and the file is never created or touched. This is how `type=float` already rejects `abc`.\n\nAdd two module-level helpers (plus `import math`):\n\n```python\ndef positive_amount(text):\n    try:\n        value = float(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f\"invalid amount: {text!r}\")\n    if not math.isfinite(value) or value <= 0:\n        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f\"amount must be a finite number greater than 0, got {text!r}\")\n    return value\n\n\ndef non_empty_category(text):\n    if not text.strip():\n        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(\"category must not be empty\")\n    return text\n```\n\nWire them up in `build_parser`:\n- `add.add_argument(\"category\", type=non_empty_category)`\n- `add.add_argument(\"amount\", type=positive_amount)`\n\n`non_empty_category` returns the text unchanged, so the existing `.strip().lower()` normalisation in `main` keeps working as before. Non-numeric amounts still exit 2 (now with a clearer message), so `test_add_rejects_non_numeric_amount` still passes. `math.isfinite` covers `nan`, `inf`, and overflow such as `1e309`. `-5` is parsed as a positional argument because the `add` subparser has no options that look like negative numbers, so it reaches the validator.\n\n## Tests in `tests/test_cli.py` (existing tests unchanged)\nAdd two tests that follow the pattern of `test_add_rejects_non_numeric_amount`: a temp dir, stderr/stdout redirected, `assertRaises(SystemExit)`, then check `code == 2` and `assertFalse(path.exists())`.\n\n1. `test_add_rejects_non_positive_or_non_finite_amount`: `subTest` over `\"0\"`, `\"-5\"`, `\"nan\"`, `\"inf\"`.\n2. `test_add_rejects_blank_category`: `subTest` over `\"\"` and `\"   \"`.\n\nEach subTest uses its own fresh temp dir/path, so \"not created\" is checked separately for every input.\n\n## Verification\n- `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` from the repo root. Tests use temp dirs only, so nothing is written outside the repo apart from the OS temp cleanup tempfile already does in the existing tests.\n- Read-only manual spot check: `python -m expenses --file <scratch path> add Food 0` should print an argparse error and exit 2, with no file created. Use a path inside the repo that is removed afterwards, or skip this and rely on the tests.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2559,"cache_read_input_tokens":41137,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2559},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9e0d1f9-7bee-4a6a-93d9-19065f2